  'Colonel John Tipton built the first house here in 1784. In February 1788 John Sevier\'s militia surrounded the farm over a dispute about enslaved people, in the clash that ended the State of Franklin. Landon Carter Haynes, later a Confederate senator, enlarged the house and weatherboarded it after 1839. The state bought the farm in 1945. Ten other buildings stand with it, among them a smokehouse, a loom house, a springhouse and a log barn, and the home of George Haynes, who was enslaved by the Haynes family.',
